        "columns": [
          {
            "text": [
              {
                "type": "heading",
                "level": 2,
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "In an intimate and expansive conversation with Achille Mbembe, we think the world from the margins, from the global south, from living memory and transgenerational pain. His thought does not arrive to order anything. It arrives to open cracks. To shake. To remember what colonialism made us forget.",
                    "type": "text"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "",
                    "type": "text"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "Joseph-Achille Mbembe, known as Achille Mbembe (born 1957), is a Cameroonian philosopher, political theorist, and intellectual. In April 2024 we met him in Girona, invited by the Ferrater Mora Chair of Contemporary Thought, and we had the opportunity to talk with him about aspects such as decolonialism, forms of governance, structural violence and spirituality. We present the conversation we kept with him, introduced by Alba Danés Boix, from Resilience.Earth and in the framework of the Balkar.Earth learning community.",
                    "type": "text"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ],
            "title": null,
            "titleHeading": null,
            "alias": "Text",
            "media": null,
