            "verticalScroller": null
          },
          {
            "text": [
              {
                "type": "heading",
                "level": 4,
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "Decolonize is to learn to exist",
                    "type": "text"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "Decolonization is not a symbolic or institutional act. It is a collective and intimate task that requires unmasking the scars of colonialism: structural racism, exploitation, denial of dignity. Mbembe tells us about psychic, cultural and spiritual decolonization. To reappropriate ourselves from our imaginary, our body, our sense of the world.",
                    "type": "text"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "heading",
                "level": 4,
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "Reimagining governance: from control to common",
                    "type": "text"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "What does it mean to rule without mastering? Mbembe invites us to rethink governance as a practice of mutual responsibility, as a way of care, not to control. Listen to forgotten voices, those who have built ways to live without hierarchies, without center, without empire. True sovereignty may not be in power, but in the ability to sustain life in common.",
                    "type": "text"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "heading",
                "level": 4,
                "children": [
                  {
                    "bold": true,
                    "text": "The violence that is not seen, but that crosses everything",
                    "type": "text"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "Structural violence does not call. It does not break out. But it erodes everything. Mbembe denounces the silent forms of oppression: hunger, precariousness, institutional racism, loss of future. Breaking with these violence involves more than reforming them: it involves imagining completely new structures.",
                    "type": "text"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "heading",
                "level": 4,
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "Spirituality and living archives",
                    "type": "text"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "Mbembe shakes us with a radical proposal: to reopen the archives of the world. Western thinking has made the planet an object. He has cut the ties between what is alive. But there are other files. The archives of the first nations, of the rooted peoples, of silences loaded with wisdom. Resonate with the cosmos, regenerate Earth and share it as a common good.",
                    "type": "text"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ],
